::ENG4_U05_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Draft: The article talks about money stuff. Which revision is most polished for an academic audience? {
=A. The article analyzes financial data related to the proposal's long-term cost.#Correct. This revision is precise, formal, and audience-appropriate.
~B. The article talks about money stuff a lot.#Incorrect. This keeps casual and vague wording.
~C. The article is really awesome about money.#Incorrect. This is informal and imprecise.
~D. The article mentions things.#Incorrect. This is too vague for publication polish.
}
::ENG4_U05_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Draft: The evidence is relevant it supports the claim. Which revision is correct and polished? {
~A. The evidence is relevant it supports the claim.#Incorrect. This remains a run-on sentence.
=B. The evidence is relevant because it supports the claim.#Correct. This fixes the sentence boundary and clarifies the relationship.
~C. The evidence, is relevant it supports the claim.#Incorrect. The comma placement does not solve the problem.
~D. Relevant evidence supports claim because.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
::ENG4_U05_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The word portable comes from the root port, meaning carry. Which revision uses the word accurately? {
~A. The portable policy was emotionally difficult.#Incorrect. Portable does not fit this context.
~B. The portable evidence caused a legal dispute.#Incorrect. Evidence is not being described as able to be carried.
=C. The portable device could be carried between work sites.#Correct. Portable means able to be carried.
~D. The portable conclusion was permanent.#Incorrect. This misuses the word.
}
::ENG4_U05_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Draft: The source is good. Which replacement is most precise if the source uses recent data and balanced reasoning? {
~A. nice.#Incorrect. Nice is vague.
~B. loud.#Incorrect. Loud does not fit the context.
~C. decorative.#Incorrect. Decorative does not describe source quality.
=D. credible.#Correct. Credible precisely describes a trustworthy source.
}
::ENG4_U05_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which final revision principle is strongest? {
=A. Revise for audience, purpose, precision, conventions, and clarity.#Correct. This captures publication polish.
~B. Make every sentence longer.#Incorrect. Length does not ensure polish.
~C. Add difficult words even if meaning changes.#Incorrect. Precision must preserve meaning.
~D. Ignore conventions if the idea is clear.#Incorrect. Conventions help readers understand meaning.
}
